# Shared Context (memory bank)

LLMs have no memory between chats. This project's memory lives in `.ai/`, is
committed to git, and is shared by every contributor and every agent. Treat it
as the source of truth — read it in, write it back.

## At the START of a task

- Read `.ai/README.md` for the map, then the files relevant to the task
  (`project.md`, `architecture.md`, `stack.md`, `conventions.md`, `progress.md`,
  the active `specs/`, and `decisions/`).
- If context and code disagree, the code is truth for *what is*, `.ai/` is truth
  for *what and why we intend*. Flag the drift and reconcile it.

## At the END of a task (before you stop)

Update whatever changed so the next person/agent starts where you left off:

- `.ai/progress.md` — what changed, current state, clear next step. Always.
- `.ai/architecture.md` / `stack.md` / `conventions.md` — if you changed design,
  stack, or conventions.
- `.ai/decisions/NNNN-*.md` — one short ADR per non-obvious decision (see template).
- `.ai/lessons.md` — if you hit a non-obvious bug or gotcha worth remembering.

## Writing rules

- Be concrete and current. Delete stale statements instead of piling caveats.
- Reference files/paths instead of pasting code, so notes don't rot.
- Keep each file focused and skimmable. Prefer bullet lists over prose.
